# Cookie banner

Explain what the site stores and why. Offer a choice only where there is an optional purpose.

## When to use

First establish what the application actually stores and why. Distinguish essential sign-in or security state from optional measurement. A banner cannot make an inaccurate description acceptable. Do not add an empty consent ritual to every local dashboard.

This documentation site uses the essential-only variant below. It combines project information with a storage explanation. Hiding it acknowledges the notice; it does not give consent to analytics or accept licence terms.

## Essential-only site notice

<%= example('core-cookie-essential') %>

The deployed notice and this example share one markup record. The site saves a cookie only when you select **Hide this notice**. The example hides for the current preview only and never saves a cookie.

The `civica_docs_notice` cookie holds the value `v1` for up to one year. It is limited to this documentation site's path, uses `SameSite=Lax` and is marked `Secure` on HTTPS. It contains no account or household information. Clearing it makes the notice appear again. A changed notice version can require a new acknowledgement.

After dismissal, keyboard focus moves to the main content. If storage is blocked, the notice hides for this page and explains that it may appear again. With JavaScript off, the notice remains readable and the dismiss button stays hidden. The footer and [Licence and site information](../community/licensing.html) remain available in every case.

